export const TIMESHEETS_BRIEF = "# Timesheets: brief for Micky\n29 Oct 2026 · Jem · Big 5 #2 (owners: Micky AU, Sam EU)\n\n## The problem in one line\nIt's purpose and ownership, not the tool. Nobody visibly uses the data, nobody owns close-out, and Productive makes the plan easier to enter than reality.\n\n## Root causes\n- No feedback loop: data nobody looks at gets entered badly.\n- Productive \"scheduled on\" and booking-based auto-tracking turn timesheets into a copy of the resource plan, which hides over-servicing and overload.\n- 14-15 hr bookings make an honest timesheet awkward.\n- Monday-lunchtime deadline means Mon-Wed work is recalled a week late. Daily entry is reliably more accurate.\n- Too many services/budget lines = too many decisions per entry.\n- Small individual rewards (half days off) turn a norm into a transaction. They don't stick.\n\n## The system (Phase 1)\n1. **Daily, ~2 min, 4:30pm local.** Agent reads your Calendar, Granola, Slack, Productive tasks and Drive edits, writes a *private draft* in Productive against a short service list, then Slack DMs: \"6h mapped, 2.5h unexplained. Confirm or correct.\" Reply by text or voice. Nothing submits without your confirmation.\n2. **Weekly brief for Wednesday capacity meeting.** Plan vs actual per person, over-hours flags, retainer burn vs month elapsed, anyone whose actuals exactly match their bookings.\n3. **Monthly / end of project close-out.** Budget vs actual, overrun services, scope changes, draft recon note for the account lead to sign off.\n4. **Make it visible.** Monthly retainer health view (green/amber/red) plus one story of a decision the data changed. That's the reward.\n\n## Pilot\n- 4 weeks, ~6 volunteers: at least 2 creatives/editors, 2 account/PMs, both offices.\n- Week 1 shadow mode (drafts generated, people log as normal, we compare). Weeks 2-4 draft-and-confirm.\n- Measure: same-day confirm %, on-time weekly %, minutes to confirm, how often drafts get edited, unexplained hours by role, a one-question \"easier/same/worse\" pulse.\n- Go/no-go at a capacity meeting before January rollout.\n- Kill if: nobody edits drafts but spot-checks find errors, sentiment goes negative, or legal says the read-scope can't work.\n\n## Phase 2 (fallback)\nDrop timesheets for salaried staff; only hourly contractors and freelancers log time. Keep moving to output/value pricing either way (track time only for those who sell time; watch gross profit per head). Use short full-detail \"calibration sprints\" when pricing new output types (e.g. Zambrero).\n\n## Watch-outs\n- **Creatives' drafts will be weakest.** Edit sessions in Adobe/Premiere leave almost no trace in Calendar or Slack. The \"unexplained hours\" line and a voice note are the fix; if that's not enough, trial a desktop tracker (e.g. Timely) for those roles only.\n- **Rubber-stamping.** A confident draft is just the new \"scheduled on\". Spot-check.\n- **Legal (get advice before the pilot):** NSW needs 14 days' written notice before computer surveillance, and an agent reading staff Slack/email likely counts. Munich: if there's a works council, it has a say over any tool *capable* of monitoring, pilots included. Germany also legally requires recording working time, so Phase 2 can't drop that in Munich.\n- Add a \"what the timesheet agent can read\" clause to the AI policy.\n\n## To check with you\n1. Which Productive plan are we on? MCP and agents look like Ultimate only. This decides how we build it.\n2. Can we switch off \"scheduled on\" suggestions and booking auto-tracking for the pilot group?\n3. Does Munich have a works council? Who's our employment-law contact in AU and DE?\n4. Your 6 pilot volunteers?\n5. Are the 14-15 hr bookings planning noise or real hours?\n6. Baseline: % of timesheets in by Monday lunch over the last 8 weeks, and % of entries that exactly match scheduled hours.\n\n## For the workshop (Ed, Micky, Alyssa, Sam)\nDecide three things: the simplified service list (~5-8 per retainer), the named close-out owner, and pilot thresholds (hours, burn %).\n";

export const REPORTING_INTAKE = `# Reporting intake: the questions
For the intake call with Stef and Fergus. Goal: leave with one report to automate first, plus the files and access to build it.

## Scope
1. When we say "reporting", which do you mean: client performance reports, internal retainer health (are we making money on it), or both? Which hurts more?
2. Walk me through every report the social and talent team sends: name, client, how often, who reads it.
3. Which one report, if it built itself tomorrow, would give you the most time back?

## How it runs today
4. Take that report: what are the steps from data to sent, and who does each one?
5. Roughly how many hours does one take, and how many go out a month across all clients? (rough is fine)
6. Where does the data come from: Meta Business Suite, TikTok, Sprout or another tool, Netflix's tools, client spreadsheets, Productive?
7. Is any of it exported by hand, screenshotted or copy-pasted? Which part do people hate most?
8. What format does it end up in: deck, Google Sheet, PDF, email? Is there a template, or does every client get its own?

## What good looks like
9. Show me the best report the team has sent. What makes it the best?
10. Which part is pure numbers, and which part needs a human: the insight, the "so what", the recommendation?
11. What do clients actually read or ask about? What do they ignore?
12. Do any clients have their own reporting rules or formats (Netflix, Zambrero, Bank First)?

## Talent
13. Is talent reporting different from social reporting: creator or influencer results, usage, rights?
14. Where does talent data live today?

## People and rules
15. Who in the team builds these now, and who should own the system once it's built?
16. Can we push any client data into Claude? Are there clients we can't touch?
17. Which logins can Jem or Claude get to (read-only is fine): Meta, TikTok, the reporting tools, Drive folders?

## First step
18. Pick one client and one report as the pilot. When is the next one due?
19. Send the last 2 or 3 versions of it, plus the raw data exports behind one of them.
20. Who signs off that the automated version is right before it goes to the client?
`;

